WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.168
-You had a wonderful week.

00:00:02.168 --> 00:00:04.470
-Yeah, exactly.
-The Emmys were just a delight.

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:05.905
I was lucky enough to see you

00:00:05.905 --> 00:00:07.674
right after you won
your first one.

00:00:07.674 --> 00:00:08.774
-Yes.

00:00:08.774 --> 00:00:10.242
-There was sort of
a backstage bar,

00:00:10.242 --> 00:00:11.510
and I'm standing
with Bill Hader,

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:13.213
and you came in the room,

00:00:13.213 --> 00:00:14.614
and everybody's
very happy for you.

00:00:14.614 --> 00:00:16.382
And Bill said, "How are you?"

00:00:16.382 --> 00:00:18.885
And you had an Emmy in your hand
and you said, "I'm really well."

00:00:18.885 --> 00:00:20.286
[ Laughter ]

00:00:20.286 --> 00:00:21.587
And I thought that was
a very honest

00:00:21.587 --> 00:00:24.223
yet also very British way
of responding to how...

00:00:24.223 --> 00:00:26.092
-"Very well, thank you."
-...your night had been going.

00:00:26.092 --> 00:00:27.426
I mean, obviously,

00:00:27.426 --> 00:00:30.296
it was beyond your wildest
dreams to win three Emmys.

00:00:30.296 --> 00:00:32.465
-Yeah. I mean...
[ Laughs ]

00:00:32.465 --> 00:00:34.033
I still,
even hearing you say it there

00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:35.868
makes me go,
"No, that didn't happen.

00:00:35.868 --> 00:00:38.171
That just happened in my head."
-Was this a situation

00:00:38.171 --> 00:00:41.040
where, looking back you,
you were just happy to be there,

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:42.841
and then it just went so beyond?

00:00:42.841 --> 00:00:44.810
-Yeah, and we had
a whole gang --

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:46.880
Like, the team that makes
"Fleabag" is quite small,

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:49.115
and we'd all come out for it,
so it just felt like we were

00:00:49.115 --> 00:00:52.118
the kind of naughty kids trying
to get in through the back door.

00:00:52.118 --> 00:00:54.053
And then we got there, and then
we were like, "Oh, no!

00:00:54.053 --> 00:00:55.488
What's happening?
This is terrible."

00:00:55.488 --> 00:00:56.890
I feel like
we were stealing them.

00:00:56.890 --> 00:00:58.658
[ Laughter ]
-And everybody reminds you --

00:00:58.658 --> 00:01:00.092
You know, they say,
even if you don't think

00:01:00.092 --> 00:01:02.062
you have a chance to win,
you have to write a speech,

00:01:02.062 --> 00:01:03.897
but no one says,
"Write three speeches."

00:01:03.897 --> 00:01:05.598
[ Laughter ]

00:01:05.598 --> 00:01:07.333
So, at the end of the night,
you really have to

00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:10.069
show your chops as a writer to
come up with new stuff to say.

00:01:10.069 --> 00:01:12.805
-Yeah, that was --
that was a horrible moment

00:01:12.805 --> 00:01:14.573
when I was like, "What if...?"

00:01:14.573 --> 00:01:16.709
And they're like,
"You don't want to tempt fate."

00:01:16.709 --> 00:01:18.244
And so I think just
repeating the jokes...

00:01:18.244 --> 00:01:19.378
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:01:19.378 --> 00:01:20.647
-Like, wow, really.

00:01:20.647 --> 00:01:22.181
It's so blurry what actually
happened that night.

00:01:22.181 --> 00:01:24.984
I mean, it was --
it was amazing.

00:01:24.984 --> 00:01:26.485
-I think, again, you --

00:01:26.485 --> 00:01:28.120
You looked very glamorous
that night,

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:29.788
and then I think
there are people that think

00:01:29.788 --> 00:01:32.058
there is something glamorous
to writing.

00:01:32.058 --> 00:01:33.492
I don't know
if you were young --

00:01:33.492 --> 00:01:34.927
When I was young,
I thought of writing

00:01:34.927 --> 00:01:36.662
as this really glamorous
endeavor that...

00:01:36.662 --> 00:01:37.897
-Yeah.
-...you do with a, you know,

00:01:37.897 --> 00:01:39.565
coffee and in a...
-Or a whiskey.

00:01:39.565 --> 00:01:42.034
-...cozy room and just --
[ Laughter ]

00:01:42.034 --> 00:01:43.303
And I thought
it was really lovely

00:01:43.303 --> 00:01:44.336
that you posted this photo.

00:01:44.336 --> 00:01:46.171
This is on your way
to the Emmys.

00:01:46.171 --> 00:01:48.874
-Yeah.
-You were writing.

00:01:48.874 --> 00:01:50.509
-Yeah.
-Which is, uh...

00:01:50.509 --> 00:01:52.144
-Yeah. No rest.
-No rest.

00:01:52.144 --> 00:01:53.646
-No rest for a writer.
-I think this is such

00:01:53.646 --> 00:01:55.114
a helpful thing
to young writers is,

00:01:55.114 --> 00:01:58.417
even Phoebe Waller-Bridge
was late with a script.

00:01:58.417 --> 00:01:59.752
Is that accurate?
If you're writing...

00:01:59.752 --> 00:02:02.955
-Oh, oh, that is not
the first time, either.

00:02:02.955 --> 00:02:04.724
[ Laughter ]
Very much.

00:02:04.724 --> 00:02:06.726
Yeah, that was honestly,
I closed --

00:02:06.726 --> 00:02:08.594
We got the deadline
just as we were pulling in,

00:02:08.594 --> 00:02:10.029
and it was like --
[ Breathes deeply ]

00:02:10.029 --> 00:02:12.465
"Okay! Let's go."
[ Laughter ]

00:02:12.465 --> 00:02:15.401
-It just speaks to, again,
the complete --

00:02:15.401 --> 00:02:16.902
It seems like people
are on the top of the world.

00:02:16.902 --> 00:02:19.839
You have so little power that,
even on the night of the Emmys,

00:02:19.839 --> 00:02:22.374
people were like,
"Where is the script, Phoebe?"

00:02:22.374 --> 00:02:24.143
-I know!
I know.

00:02:24.143 --> 00:02:26.379
It's actually --
And it's kind of -- It was --

00:02:26.379 --> 00:02:28.814
I was writing the end
of the book of the scripts

00:02:28.814 --> 00:02:30.483
that's coming out,
and I was writing all these,

00:02:30.483 --> 00:02:33.018
like, emotional bits about
the people who had, you know,

00:02:33.018 --> 00:02:34.520
really influenced it.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:36.222
And so I actually got myself
really riled up

00:02:36.222 --> 00:02:37.456
before I went there,
'cause I was like,

00:02:37.456 --> 00:02:39.659
"My director was just
such an amazing guy."

00:02:39.659 --> 00:02:40.927
[ Laughter ]

00:02:40.927 --> 00:02:43.095
So, I was really --
I was really in the right place.

00:02:43.095 --> 00:02:45.131
But it is -- Yeah, I mean,
it's right up to the line.

00:02:45.131 --> 00:02:46.699
-And that is --
So you're writing, sort of,

00:02:46.699 --> 00:02:48.667
this is a collection of all the
"Fleabag" scripts

00:02:48.667 --> 00:02:50.103
that's gonna be for a book.
-Yeah.

00:02:50.103 --> 00:02:52.171
-Now I would assume then,
after you win three Emmys,

00:02:52.171 --> 00:02:53.306
the publisher probably says,

00:02:53.306 --> 00:02:55.541
"Hey, you want
to rewrite the ending?"

00:02:55.541 --> 00:02:58.143
'Cause, I mean, you really did
add on a fantastic post-script

00:02:58.143 --> 00:03:00.346
to the entire thing.
-What? What do you mean?

00:03:00.346 --> 00:03:02.114
-Well, the book.
I feel like now, like,

00:03:02.114 --> 00:03:04.050
in the part of the book that
you have the "Fleabag" scripts,

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:06.285
I feel like you should add,
"Also, right as I finish this,

00:03:06.285 --> 00:03:07.720
I went out and won three Emmys."
-Oh, no.

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:09.188
That would be amazing.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:03:09.188 --> 00:03:10.923
-Yeah, yeah.
I mean, as I was finishing it,

00:03:10.923 --> 00:03:12.591
I was like, "And I'm on my way
to the Emmys."

00:03:12.591 --> 00:03:15.127
[ Laughter ]
But yeah.

00:03:15.127 --> 00:03:17.563
-I know you had said that
this was the end of "Fleabag,"

00:03:17.563 --> 00:03:20.133
and it certainly has
as wonderful an ending

00:03:20.133 --> 00:03:22.902
as you could ever want
for a piece of television.

00:03:22.902 --> 00:03:25.337
But I've also heard you say
that maybe years from now,

00:03:25.337 --> 00:03:27.640
you might revisit the character.
Is there something...?

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:31.210
-Yeah, I quite like the idea
of coming back to her when --

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:33.346
Well, me, when I'm 50.
-Right.

00:03:33.346 --> 00:03:35.314
-'Cause I feel like
she would've had, you know,

00:03:35.314 --> 00:03:37.683
more life then, and God knows
what she would have got up to.

00:03:37.683 --> 00:03:40.019
And actually
seeing a character like that

00:03:40.019 --> 00:03:42.688
in her later stage of life,
I think, is exciting.

00:03:42.688 --> 00:03:45.491
But I think, for now,
she's been through enough.

00:03:45.491 --> 00:03:46.825
-Yeah, she's --
[ Laughter ]

00:03:46.825 --> 00:03:49.529
-We got to let her go.
-Let's let her have some rest.

00:03:49.529 --> 00:03:51.898
-Yeah.
-Well-deserved rest.

00:03:51.898 --> 00:03:53.966
"SNL" this week, you're at the
very beginning of the week.

00:03:53.966 --> 00:03:55.167
-Yeah.
-Is this a show

00:03:55.167 --> 00:03:57.036
you had any sense of,
growing up in England?

00:03:57.036 --> 00:03:59.605
-Yeah, but only really
through YouTube and stuff.

00:03:59.605 --> 00:04:02.308
I mean, everyone was sharing
all the skits and the clips

00:04:02.308 --> 00:04:04.644
and everything, but we didn't --
it was never aired in the U.K.

00:04:04.644 --> 00:04:07.413
So I didn't know the madness
that it is

00:04:07.413 --> 00:04:11.283
and how you create it and how
it's written and how fast.

00:04:11.283 --> 00:04:12.818
You know, we just thought
it was a sketch show, like,

00:04:12.818 --> 00:04:14.487
that you have time
to prepare for.

00:04:14.487 --> 00:04:16.021
-Right, right.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:16.021 --> 00:04:18.925
-And turns out you don't.
-You really don't.

00:04:18.925 --> 00:04:22.261
I mean, because at this point --
Again, so it's Tuesday night.

00:04:22.261 --> 00:04:24.663
You have a show on Saturday,
and it's safe to say --

00:04:24.663 --> 00:04:25.797
I know from experience --

00:04:25.797 --> 00:04:27.499
you haven't seen
a single word yet.

00:04:27.499 --> 00:04:28.501
You haven't --
-No.

00:04:28.501 --> 00:04:29.635
-Yeah.
-No.

00:04:29.635 --> 00:04:32.838
I mean, when do they do it?
[ Laughter ]

00:04:32.838 --> 00:04:34.140
Yeah, I've heard that today's

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:35.341
the kind of calm
before the storm.

00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:36.743
-Yeah, I think tomorrow --
I'm gonna check in

00:04:36.743 --> 00:04:38.177
with you tomorrow just to see
where you're at.

00:04:38.177 --> 00:04:39.912
-Yeah. Can you just help me?
Can you just help me with it?

00:04:39.912 --> 00:04:41.581
-Yeah, I'll try a little bit.
Yeah.

